/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the framing mechanisms used at the Data Link Layer. Detail Byte-Oriented Framing \(Byte Stuffing\) and Bit-Oriented Framing \(Bit Stuffing in HDLC\) with comprehensive illustrative examples.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Functional Necessity of Framing:) Tj
T*
(   The Physical layer transmits an uninterpreted, continuous stream of raw binary pulses over the) Tj
T*
(   communication channel.) Tj
T*
(   The Data Link Layer must delineate discrete frame boundaries so the receiver can recognize) Tj
T*
(   where each frame begins and terminates,) Tj
T*
(   extract protocol headers, compute error checksums, and forward data payloads to the Network) Tj
T*
(   layer.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Byte-Oriented Framing and Byte Stuffing \(Character Stuffing\):) Tj
T*
(   - Mechanism: Frames are viewed as collections of bytes \(characters\). Special control characters) Tj
T*
(   designate frame boundaries:) Tj
T*
(   * DLE STX \(Data Link Escape - Start of Text\) marks frame beginning.) Tj
T*
(   * DLE ETX \(Data Link Escape - End of Text\) marks frame termination.) Tj
T*
(   - The Transparency Problem: If the arbitrary user binary payload naturally contains the byte) Tj
T*
(   pattern DLE ETX \(e.g., in a binary image or executable\),) Tj
T*
(   the receiver would prematurely truncate the frame and misinterpret subsequent data.) Tj
T*
(   - Byte Stuffing Solution: Whenever the transmitter encounters an accidental DLE character in) Tj
T*
(   the payload data, it automatically inserts an extra ESCAPE \(DLE\) byte immediately preceding it.) Tj
T*
(   - Receiver Behavior: The receiver scans the incoming byte stream. When it detects a double DLE) Tj
T*
(   \(DLE DLE\), it strips off the first stuffed DLE and passes the second DLE as authentic payload) Tj
T*
(   data.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Bit-Oriented Framing and Bit Stuffing \(HDLC / SDLC\):) Tj
T*
(   - Mechanism: Frames are treated as arbitrary bit strings regardless of byte alignment. A) Tj
T*
(   universal 8-bit Flag Sequence \(01111110, or 0x7E in hex\) delimits both the start and end of) Tj
T*
(   every frame.) Tj
T*
(   - The Transparency Problem: If the payload data contains six consecutive 1s \(01111110\), the) Tj
T*
(   receiver would falsely register a premature end-of-frame flag.) Tj
T*
(   - Bit Stuffing Algorithm \(Transmitter Rule\):) Tj
T*
(   Whenever the transmitter detects FIVE consecutive 1s \(11111\) in the outgoing bit stream \(after) Tj
T*
(   the opening flag\), it AUTOMATICALLY STUFFS A '0' bit immediately after the fifth 1, regardless) Tj
T*
(   of the subsequent bit.) Tj
T*
(   - Receiver Destuffing Algorithm:) Tj
T*
(   The receiver continuously monitors the incoming bit sequence. When it encounters five) Tj
T*
(   consecutive 1s:) Tj
T*
(   * Case 1: If the 6th bit is a 0, it is a stuffed bit! The receiver automatically removes) Tj
T*
(   \(discards\) the 0 and preserves the original 11111 data stream.) Tj
T*
(   * Case 2: If the 6th bit is a 1 and the 7th bit is a 0 \(01111110\), it is the legitimate Frame) Tj
T*
(   Delimiter Flag!) Tj
T*
(   * Case 3: If seven or more consecutive 1s occur, an abort condition or transmission error is) Tj
T*
(   signaled.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
