(   stations to recognize the collision.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Truncated Binary Exponential Backoff Algorithm:) Tj
T*
(   To prevent immediate repeated collisions upon re-sensing, each colliding station executes) Tj
T*
(   exponential backoff:) Tj
T*
(   - Let k be the number of collisions experienced by the current frame \(attempt counter\).) Tj
T*
(   - Set exponent variable n = min\(k, 10\).) Tj
T*
(   - The station randomly picks an integer r uniformly from the discrete set: r in {0, 1, 2, ...,) Tj
T*
(   2^n - 1}.) Tj
T*
(   - The station waits backoff time T_backoff = r * Slot_Time \(where Slot Time = 512 bit times =) Tj
T*
(   51.2 us at 10 Mbps\).) Tj
T*
(   - Range Expansion with Collisions:) Tj
T*
(   * After 1st collision \(k=1\): r in {0, 1}.) Tj
T*
(   * After 2nd collision \(k=2\): r in {0, 1, 2, 3}.) Tj
T*
(   * After 3rd collision \(k=3\): r in {0, 1, ..., 7}.) Tj
T*
(   * After 10th collision \(k=10\): r in {0, 1, ..., 1023}.) Tj
T*
(   * Maximum cap: For k between 10 and 15, range remains fixed at 0 to 1023.) Tj
T*
(   * After 16 consecutive collisions: The station permanently aborts transmission and signals an) Tj
T*
(   excessive collision error to upper layers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 4:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Derive the relationship between minimum frame size, channel transmission speed, and maximum network physical cable distance in Ethernet CSMA/CD. Calculate maximum cable distance for 10 Mbps Ethernet with 64-byte minimum frame size.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Mathematical Condition for Valid Collision Detection:) Tj
T*
(   A transmitting station MUST be able to detect a collision while it is STILL TRANSMITTING its) Tj
T*
(   frame.) Tj
T*
(   Consider the worst-case geometric scenario: Stations A and B are separated by maximum cable) Tj
T*
(   distance d with one-way propagation delay T_prop.) Tj
T*
(   - Station A begins transmitting frame of length L bits at speed R bps. Transmission time T_tx =) Tj
T*
(   L / R.) Tj
T*
(   - The signal propagates along the cable and arrives at Station B at time t = T_prop - epsilon.) Tj
T*
(   - Station B senses channel idle just before the signal arrives and starts transmitting.) Tj
T*
(   - A collision occurs immediately at B.) Tj
T*
(   - The collision wave must propagate all the way BACK to Station A, arriving at time t = 2 *) Tj
T*
(   T_prop.) Tj
T*
(   - For Station A to realize its own frame collided, Station A must still be actively) Tj
T*
(   transmitting when the collision wave returns:) Tj
T*
(   T_tx >= 2 * T_prop.) Tj
T*
(   \( L_min / R \) >= 2 * \( d_max / v \), where v is signal propagation velocity in the cable.) Tj
T*
(   - Fundamental Formula: L_min >= 2 * R * \( d_max / v \).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Numerical Evaluation for Classic 10 Mbps Ethernet:) Tj
T*
(   - Given: R = 10 Mbps = 10 * 10^6 bps; Minimum frame size L_min = 64 bytes = 512 bits; Signal) Tj
T*
(   velocity v = 2 * 10^8 m/s \(approx 0.67c\).) Tj
T*
(   - Maximum Round-Trip Time allowed: 2 * T_prop <= L_min / R = 512 bits / \(10 * 10^6 bps\) = 51.2) Tj
T*
(   microseconds.) Tj
T*
(   - One-way propagation limit: T_prop <= 51.2 us / 2 = 25.6 microseconds.) Tj
T*
(   - Deducting repeater and transceiver hardware delays \(~ 20 us allocated for repeaters across 4) Tj
T*
(   hubs\):) Tj
T*
(   Leaves ~ 5.6 microseconds for pure cable transit.) Tj
T*
(   - Maximum pure physical cable distance: d_max = v * T_prop = \(2 * 10^8 m/s\) * \(25.6 * 10^-6 s\)) Tj
T*
(   = 5,120 meters.) Tj
T*
ET

